# Agent-ready rubric — the refine `ready` node's self-check ({{ORG_NAME}})

This is a **rubric**, not an agent. `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_REFINE}}` applies it at the
`ready` node, in the engineer's main session, to the **ticket as recorded in the
tracker** (description + comments), not to what the session remembers. A criterion that
was discussed but never written to the ticket is deficient: `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` and its
graph-agents read only the tracker.

Each criterion is checkable: it passes or it is deficient, and each maps to one of the
wiki operating model's refine outputs and to the refine node that produces it. The
operating model is the authority. If it names a refine output this rubric does not
cover, check the ticket against that output too and report the rubric as stale.

## Criteria

| # | Passes when the ticket shows… | Operating model output | Node |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | The need as `a <user> needs to <do X>`, plus the existing need it duplicates or sits under (or "none found" and where you searched) | user story; user need placed among existing needs | `need` |
| 2 | A validation/acceptance test: concrete, observable, and failing today | validation / acceptance test | `validation` |
| 3 | The test is **signed**: a tracker comment from product saying it proves the need, or, on an Org-scope ticket with no product owner, the owning engineer's attestation naming who signed | product signs off the validation test | `validation` |
| 4 | Acceptance criteria that are concrete and testable, each naming the test that will prove it | validation / acceptance test | `validation` |
| 5 | The architecture: the repos touched (each in the workspace repo index, or flagged as new), the patterns, and the interfaces between them | proposed architecture | `architecture` |
| 6 | User-facing SLOs that name who the user is; every target has a wiki baseline or is marked as a guessed threshold | SLOs | `slos` |
| 7 | A security analysis of both the problem and the solution surface (data handled, authn/authz, secrets, new exposure) | cybersecurity analysis | `cybersec` |
| 8 | For each touched repo under design controls, the NEED → REQ → SPEC → test traceability the work must produce; otherwise "n/a" with the reason | the trace the {{VERB_GATE}} gate checks | `trace` |
| 9 | Dependencies and deploy order, the rollout (feature flag, canary criteria, rollback triggers), and the analytics events that feed the SLOs | beyond the numbered steps: what each `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` task carries (dependencies, feature-flag instructions) | `deps` |
| 10 | A sketch: one line per repo × concern, every acceptance criterion covered by at least one line | refined story, broken down enough for a swarm | `sketch` |
| 11 | No PHI, PII or secrets in any refinement artifact or `REFINE-STATE` comment | the wiki's compliance guardrail | the node that wrote it |

Criteria 2 and 3 are the forcing function: without a signed validation test the ticket is
not agent-ready, whatever else passes. That test is what `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` drives to green.

## The verdict

- **PASS** (every criterion passes) → advance to `mark`. This rubric never applies the
  `agent-ready` label; the engineer approves that at the `mark` gate.
- **DEFICIENT** → list each failing criterion as `#<n> <node>: <what is missing>`, and
  report `deficiencies=<count>`. Return to the earliest deficient node. Vague findings
  ("needs more detail") are not actionable; say what would satisfy the criterion.
